>
  <section id="cover">
    <h2>What does an AI readiness assessment cover?</h2>
    <p>It covers the work itself: who does what, how long each step takes, where things wait,
       and which steps a machine could do better than a person. The output is a decision for
       each team, not a score out of ten.</p>
    <p>Zaaheen is an independent AI consultancy based in Dubai that assesses businesses across
       the UAE, Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman on site. We sell and build no
       tools, and our team spent twenty years in corporate operations before doing this work.</p>
    <p>Here is what our <a href="/consulting/">AI readiness assessment in Dubai and across the
       Gulf</a> covers, step by step:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>A free 30-minute call.</strong> You tell us about your business and what is
          not working. We tell you honestly whether an assessment is worth it for you.</li>
      <li><strong>We walk every role, on site.</strong> From reception to the managing director,
          we sit with the people doing the work, watch how it really gets done and time each
          step.</li>
      <li><strong>We map and score it.</strong> Every process becomes a map with timed steps,
          hand-offs and waiting points. Each step is scored on volume, repetition, rules, data
          and the cost of a mistake.</li>
      <li><strong>Report and read-out.</strong> A written report and a meeting with your team:
          where AI helps, where it does not, what to do first, what it costs and when it pays
          back.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Each team gets one of four verdicts, with the reasons behind it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>AI now:</strong> work that reads, writes, sorts or answers, at volume, where
          the data already exists.</li>
      <li><strong>Automate without AI:</strong> fixed rules and clean inputs, where a form, a
          workflow or a link between two systems does it cheaper and safer. Our guide to
          <a href="/learn/ai-vs-automation/">AI vs automation</a> explains the difference.</li>
      <li><strong>Fix the process first:</strong> too many exceptions, unclear ownership or
          missing data. AI on a broken process only makes it break faster.</li>
      <li><strong>Leave as it is:</strong> low volume, or expert judgement where a mistake is
          costly. Some work should stay with your people.</li>
    </ul>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-assessment-visit"
      from="Front desk, Dubai"
      to="Written report"
      fact="Walk-in queries logged"
      hub="On-site visit"
      hubNote="every role, timed"
      saved="Verdict given"
      caption="We sit with every team on site, and each gets a verdict with its reasons."
    />

    <p>For every recommendation the report gives the effort, a cost range, the hours it saves
       each month, when it pays back, and where a person must stay in charge. If you would rather
       look for candidates yourself first, our guide on
       <a href="/learn/where-ai-helps-your-business/">where AI helps your business</a> shows the
       method.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="ready">
    <h2>How ready are Gulf businesses for AI?</h2>
    <p>Most GCC organisations plan to use AI, but far fewer have the data, the rules or the way
       of working to make it pay. That gap is what an assessment is for.</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-gcc-readiness"
      title="GCC organisations: AI plans vs readiness to scale, per Roland Berger"
      measure="% of organisations"
      max={100}
      bars={[
        { label: 'AI in strategic plans', value: 80, note: '% (nearly)' },
        { label: 'Enterprise-wide data strategy', value: 33, note: '% (one-third)' },
        { label: 'AI ethics or compliance board', value: 28, note: '%' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: 'Roland Berger Middle East report, 10 February 2026. Sample size not published; "one-third" is plotted as 33.', href: ROLAND_BERGER }}
    />

    <p>In its February 2026 report, Roland Berger found that nearly 80% of GCC organisations have
       AI in their strategic plans, but only one-third have an enterprise-wide data strategy and
       fewer than one in three have the operating model to scale it. Its first recommended action
       was organisation-wide AI readiness assessments.</p>
    <p>The same pattern shows up in Deloitte's June 2026 survey of more than 3,200 leaders in 24
       countries: 84% of organisations had not yet redesigned jobs or workflows around AI
       (<a href={DELOITTE}>Deloitte Middle East, 4 June 2026</a>). Boston Consulting Group put it
       more bluntly in October 2024: 74% of companies had yet to show tangible value from AI, and
       leaders put about 70% of their effort into people and processes, not technology
       (<a href={BCG}>BCG, 1,000 executives in 59 countries</a>).</p>
    <p>Whether you run a 40-person firm in Abu Dhabi or a large company in Riyadh, the lesson is
       the same: the problem is rarely the technology. It is the way the work is done. Our article
       on <a href="/learn/why-ai-projects-fail/">why AI projects fail</a> goes through the named
       studies.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="quiz">
    <h2>Is a free online quiz or "free AI audit" enough?</h2>
    <p>A free quiz is a fair starting point, but it scores your own answers, and a free audit from
       a company that builds AI is usually the first step towards selling you its own work. Neither
       sees how your people really work.</p>
    <p>Many searches in Dubai and the UAE are for an "AI audit for my business". The word is
       understandable, but an AI readiness assessment is not a financial audit, an assurance report
       or a certification of any AI system. It is an operational review of where AI can help.</p>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Online quiz</th><th scope="col">Free audit from a company that builds AI</th><th scope="col">On-site independent assessment</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who looks at the work</th><td>Nobody: you answer questions</td><td>Often a short call or a form</td><td>Someone who sits with each team</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What it measures</th><td>Your own view of readiness</td><td>Where their product could fit</td><td>Timed steps, hand-offs and waiting points</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Can it say "don't use AI here"?</th><td>Rarely</td><td>Rarely: it is how they win work</td><td>Yes: "Leave as it is" is a real verdict</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What you get</th><td>A score</td><td>A proposal for their build work</td><td>A written report, costs and payback, and a read-out</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost</th><td>Free</td><td>Free, paid back through the build</td><td>A fixed fee, quoted in writing</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A quiz is useful if you want to know whether to look further. If you want to know what to do
       on Monday morning, someone has to watch the work.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="independent">
    <h2>How do you check an AI consultant is independent?</h2>
    <p>Ask three plain questions, and get the answers in writing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Do you sell, resell or build any AI product, software or hardware?</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Do you take commissions, referral fees or kickbacks from any provider?</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Are you a partner or reseller of any provider?</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>If the answer to any of these is yes, their advice may still be good, but it is not
       independent. An adviser who earns from a tool has a reason to recommend it.</p>
    <p>Our answer to all three is no. Our only income is the fee you pay us. If we name a product,
       it is because it fits your work, and you buy it directly from its maker. The longer
       checklist, including red flags and who actually does the work, is in our guide on
       <a href="/learn/how-to-choose-an-ai-consultant-uae/">how to choose an AI consultant in the
       UAE</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>What about data protection in the UAE and Saudi Arabia?</h2>
    <p>Your adviser will see how your business runs, so data protection should be agreed before the
       first visit, not after. This section is general information, not legal advice.</p>
    <p>In the UAE, personal data is covered by the Personal Data Protection Law, Federal Decree-Law
       No. 45 of 2021, alongside the separate rules of free zones such as DIFC and ADGM. Saudi
       Arabia has its own Personal Data Protection Law, and Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait and Oman each
       have their own rules too. Take advice from a lawyer on what applies to your business.</p>
    <p>What you can ask of any adviser is simple:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A confidentiality agreement signed before the first visit.</li>
      <li>Roles and processes recorded, not people's names or personal details.</li>
      <li>A clear date when notes are deleted.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>That is how we work: we sign the agreement first, record roles rather than people, and
       delete our notes 90 days after the report unless you ask us in writing to keep them for a
       follow-on project.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="time">
    <h2>How long does an AI assessment take, and what does it cost?</h2>
    <p>For a small firm, it usually takes two to three weeks from the first visit to the read-out.
       Larger businesses with more teams and sites take longer, and we agree the timeline in writing
       before we start.</p>
    <p>On cost, we publish no price list, because every business is different. After the free call,
       once we know how many teams and sites are involved, we send a written quote: one fixed price
       in UAE dirhams, plus VAT where it applies, set out in full, showing what each stage covers. There are no hidden costs,
       and nothing is added later without your written agreement.</p>
    <p>The call is free and commits you to nothing. The assessment itself is a paid service. Before
       spending on any AI tool, it helps to work out the payback in plain numbers; our guide to
       <a href="/learn/ai-roi-before-you-buy/">AI ROI before you buy</a> shows how.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>What makes Zaaheen's AI readiness assessment different?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>On site, every role.</strong> From reception to the managing director, timing
          each step.</li>
      <li><strong>Independent.</strong> We do not sell, resell or build tools, and we take no
          commissions or referral fees. Our only income is the fee you pay us.</li>
      <li><strong>An honest verdict for every team.</strong> Including "Fix the process first" and
          "Leave as it is", because some work should stay with your people.</li>
      <li><strong>Twenty years in operations.</strong> Our team spent twenty years in customer
          service, back office, sales and team management.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbers you can act on.</strong> Effort, a cost range, hours saved each month and
          payback for every recommendation.</li>
      <li><strong>A clear price.</strong> A free 30-minute call, then a fixed written quote with no
          hidden costs, plus VAT where it applies.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We work with businesses of twenty people or more, from Dubai and Abu Dhabi to Riyadh, Doha,
       Kuwait City, Manama and Muscat. You can read <a href="/consulting/">how our AI Readiness
       Assessment works</a> in full.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>What else do people ask about AI readiness assessments?</h2>

    <h3>Do you work in Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman?</h3>
    <p>Yes. We are based in Dubai and work with businesses across the Gulf: the UAE, Saudi Arabia
       (KSA), Qatar, Kuwait, Bahrain and Oman. The work is done on site, so we come to your office,
       whether it is in Abu Dhabi, Riyadh or Doha.</p>

    <h3>Is this an AI audit?</h3>
    <p>Not in the financial sense. It is an operational assessment of how work gets done and where
       AI can help. It is not a financial audit, an assurance report or a certification of any AI
       system.</p>

    <h3>What happens to what we show you?</h3>
    <p>We sign a confidentiality agreement before the first visit. We record roles and processes,
       not people's names or personal details. Notes are deleted 90 days after the report, unless
       you ask us in writing to keep them for a follow-on project.</p>

    <h3>Do you earn anything from the providers you recommend?</h3>
    <p>No. We have no affiliate programme, referral fees, commissions or kickbacks from any
       provider. Our only income is the fee you pay us, so we work for you.</p>

    <h3>What do we get at the end?</h3>
    <p>A written report and a read-out meeting with your team. Every team gets a verdict with its
       reasons, and every recommendation comes with the effort, a cost range, hours saved each month
       and when it pays back.</p>

    <p>More answers, including how payment and cancellation work, are in the
       <a href="/consulting/#questions">questions on our assessment page</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
